 -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef4ac66-d953-5fba-ac6a-17597fb2834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315208} {:answer/id #uuid "28317ab6-956f-51f4-9c07-7893dc4262d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315349} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9c2bd9-cb6c-5ceb-9adb-bb9bf53c2214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315732} {:answer/id #uuid "8cada019-11c7-5af3-83c1-8512283cb4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315902} {:answer/id #uuid "c19aff71-14a7-558f-9b3f-f6060cbbd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c9c2bd9-cb6c-5ceb-9adb-bb9bf53c2214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315732} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb47da4-af36-516e-a2d7-8a35d392e1aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "113a385d-0512-508f-a50c-e45bc26c3c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34300" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56b387d2-a8ae-5558-8231-cc17907bc640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315618} {:answer/id #uuid "8525addc-9c85-5ee6-8670-56e2de81448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315859} {:answer/id #uuid "c265259f-5a56-59c2-a99d-4927b560a426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316213} {:answer/id #uuid "ecca11f7-62a6-51ce-9140-c76055be2baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316434} {:answer/id #uuid "fb14a651-51be-513b-bfe6-7a0658ea2cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecca11f7-62a6-51ce-9140-c76055be2baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316434} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7522ee3-25f0-51ae-8e3a-74563c620267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "028630ef-e1bc-5d42-ac61-af7d94939f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34318" :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebeebae27d24ae4dd342b9cf65f93fe58e77aa8dcd99a7cde0880f68378862fa.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71f49cca-acfc-5a6f-adec-d56cd8c4188f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315765} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb5bdbe-5c14-56e2-baac-3c15294b8a3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315820} {:answer/id #uuid "9bed0902-a4ee-513d-be6c-ca5568cc027d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315988} {:answer/id #uuid "b87a8264-9168-5a25-a6cc-a7d1a0aafdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316164} {:answer/id #uuid "d45a644e-c9e7-5144-95bb-fbbcf3374c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f49cca-acfc-5a6f-adec-d56cd8c4188f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315765} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a2c6ab-2d7f-566d-b0ca-7d2a78b0bff9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92efc5e4-6fcf-5867-b774-9cceef4cda9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34327" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139a1cef-2533-5975-aee8-34b92cbec2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321304} {:answer/id #uuid "2c42cb6b-3a43-5e40-90aa-9faa026d0a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321488} {:answer/id #uuid "437716e0-b97a-50de-ab6e-2583496fbbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321679} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba5e528-0a67-5866-8b57-6e4bd76ff209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321743} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aa45ec-c5e8-5675-96e4-bdcb7d16c7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ba5e528-0a67-5866-8b57-6e4bd76ff209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321743} :answer-field/id #uuid "b59196ae-299c-521d-82cd-c6982e348c6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d3ff8e-37f2-5365-be21-7d8861823943" :question/math-academy-id "q-35595" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3b62554600601401d14d3b6758a3c350cf8cddc3788e79a3ba456a5a19c60a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baba48d0-d9fb-5842-84e6-6919f8e36cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407328} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd0836e-e6f4-5b58-bfb5-c8dec7e9a94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407330} {:answer/id #uuid "f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407331} {:answer/id #uuid "f67abfd4-642f-5614-876f-390eb3d1595b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407332} {:answer/id #uuid "fe963c24-be42-5cfe-a77e-8b918c0ffa49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16a724b-c1f8-5ad0-8225-b9af9742e1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407331} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c15890f-719b-5cd8-8053-341247002966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74214" :question/problem "![](images/42/42e0a9a9e987c1c56a335f29cf939e4f03d496fad148f148c32377893dbe7c13.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3171ea47-31ac-55ef-8e65-7c8cc13d17db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339563} {:answer/id #uuid "4780f3c7-7445-5e20-b679-8d09700f5b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339761} {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340063} {:answer/id #uuid "7458caf1-0896-52f4-b93d-16c6705c4441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340166} {:answer/id #uuid "962e504c-ea04-57e3-acb7-34e1af23490d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69308677-f0aa-523b-9be3-7371c6dc3d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340063} :answer-field/id #uuid "df4036f4-0b20-53ba-89f9-e6eee6e466b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb81e4a8-c5a7-59da-b168-b278b747b24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74255" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}<\\frac{1}{2}\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5x-y-z=0\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:5-x-z=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨\\frac{1}{4},0,0⟩$ is in $A$, since\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nbut, $4u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $5x-y-z=0$ since $5\\cdot 0-0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $5-x-z=0$ since $5-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
